    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in relation to a trustee's authority to recant the invasion of a trust and the creation of a new trust",
    "basePrintNo": "S5191",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5191-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §10-6.6, EPT L",
    "lawSection": "Estates, Powers and Trusts Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5191\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the estates, powers and trusts law, in\nrelation to a trustee's authority to recant the invasion of a trust\nand the creation of a new trust\n\nThis is one in a series of measures being introduced at the request of\nthe Chief Administrative Judge upon the recommendation of her\nSurrogate's Court Advisory Committee.\n\nEPTL 10-6.6, the decanting statute, does not now provide for a\ntrustee's authority to recant the invasion of a trust and creation of\na new trust within the thirty day period before the decanting takes\neffect. The issues are: whether a trustee may reverse course after the\nnotice of the decanting has been served; what form this recanting\nshould take; and whether notice of the recanting must be served upon\nthe persons interested in the invaded trust and new trust.\n\nEPTL 10-6.6 permits a trustee to decant to a new trust and requires\nthat notice of the decanting be served upon all persons interested in\nthe invaded trust and new trust, inter alia (EPTL 10-6.6 (j)).\nPursuant to paragraph (j) of this section, the exercise of the power\nmust be in writing and, absent consent from the persons interested, is\neffective thirty days after the date of service of the instrument as\nprovided under subparagraph (2) of paragraph (j). Subparagraph (2)\nsets forth the content of the notice which must be served upon those\npersons interested in the invaded trust and new trust. Where consent\nis obtained, the effective date is the date of the consent.\n\nThe initial question is: when does exercise of the power to decant\nbecome irrevocable? As a matter of simple reasoning, the decanting\nshould be revocable until its effective date, since it is ineffective\nuntil that time. EPTL 10-6.6 (d) provides that \"(a)n exercise of the\npower to invade trust principal under paragraphs (b) and (c) of this\nsection shall be considered the exercise of a special power of\nappointment as defined in section 10-3.2 of this article.\" EPTL 10-9.1\n(b) provides that the exercise of a special power becomes irrevocable\nwhen the donor \"manifests his or her intention that its exercise be\nirrevocable.\" The provision in EPTL 10-6.6 that a decanting becomes\neffective in thirty days in effect represents a statutory directive\nthat the decanting becomes irrevocable at that time. Accordingly, it\nis recommended that paragraph (j) be amended to add the following as\nthe last sentence:\n\n\"The exercise of the power is irrevocable on the effective date,\neither 30 days following service of the notice or the effective date\nas set forth in the written consent.\"\n\nFrom this, it follows that the trustee would have the authority to\nrecant the decanting before it becomes irrevocable. Where a trustee\nhas served the requisite notice of decanting, such notice must be\nserved upon the same persons and in the same manner. To make clear\nsuch result, it is recommended that the statute be amended to add a\nnew subparagraph (7) to paragraph (j) to read as follows:\n\n\"(7) Prior to the effective date as provided herein, a trustee may\nrevoke the exercise of the power to invade to a new trust. Where a\n\n\ntrustee served notice of the exercise of the power pursuant to\nsubdivision (2) herein, the trustee shall serve notice of the\nrevocation of the exercise of the power to persons interested in the\ninvaded trust and the appointed trust by registered or certified mail,\nreturn receipt requested, or by personal delivery or in any other\nmanner directed by the court having jurisdiction over the invaded\ntrust. Where the notice of the exercise of the power was filed with\nthe court, the trustee shall file the notice of revocation of the\nexercise of the power with such court.\"\n\nThe proposed amendments raise another question, i.e., whether the\nrequirement that the notice be filed with the court creates an\nadditional filing requirement where a trustee recants the exercise of\nthe power? To limit such instances, it is recommended that\nsubparagraph (6) of paragraph (j) be amended to add the following\nunderlined text as follows:\n\n\"(6) A copy of the instrument exercising the power shall be kept with\nthe records of the invaded trust and within twenty days of the\neffective date, the original shall be filed in the court having\njurisdiction over the invaded trust....\"\n\nThis measure would amend paragraph (j) of EPTL 10-6.6 to resolve the\nuncertainty whether a trustee may recant a decanting during the thirty\nday period before the decanting takes effect. The amendments make\nclear that the trustee has such authority and provide the procedure to\ndo so. The amendments address solely the right to recant during the\nthirty-day period and do not authorize a trustee to amend an invasion\nand creation of a trust during the thirty-day period.  Following the\neffective date of the decanting, the new trust is irrevocable and may\nbe changed only by a new exercise of the power to invade and creation\nof a new trust.\n\nThis measure, which would have no fiscal impact, would take effect\nimmediately and apply to all estates and living trusts.\n\n2014 Legislative History:\n\nOCA 2014-102\nAssembly 9761 (M. of A. Braunstein) (Passed)\n\n",
